-          "claim": "For full-column-rank $Y=[y_1\\ \\cdots\\ y_r]$, its columns are linearly independent. Apply Gram\u2013Schmidt [F1], writing $u_j=y_j-\\sum_{i<j}(q_i^{\\mathsf T}y_j)q_i$ and $q_j=u_j/\\|u_j\\|_2$. The residuals are nonzero, so $Q=[q_1\\ \\cdots\\ q_r]$ has orthonormal columns. Set $R=Q^{\\mathsf T}Y$. For $i>j$, $q_i$ is orthogonal to $\\operatorname{span}(y_1,\\ldots,y_j)$, so $R_{ij}=0$; and $R_{jj}=q_j^{\\mathsf T}y_j=\\|u_j\\|_2>0$. Since each $y_j$ lies in $\\operatorname{span}(q_1,\\ldots,q_j)$, $QR=Y$. This constructs a reduced QR factorization with positive diagonal. To check uniqueness, if $Y=Q_1R_1=Q_2R_2$ are two such factorizations, then $Q_2^{\\mathsf T}Q_1=R_2R_1^{-1}$ is orthogonal and upper triangular with positive diagonal. Its first column has only its first entry, which must be $1$ by unit norm and positivity; orthogonality zeros the rest of its first row. Induction on the remaining block makes it $I_r$, so $Q_1=Q_2$ and $R_1=R_2$. For the polar factor $U=Y(Y^{\\mathsf T}Y)^{-1/2}$, symmetry of the root and its defining square give $U^{\\mathsf T}U=(Y^{\\mathsf T}Y)^{-1/2}(Y^{\\mathsf T}Y)(Y^{\\mathsf T}Y)^{-1/2}=I_r$. Hence both outputs have orthonormal columns. If $Y$ is replaced by $YQ_0$ for $Q_0\\in\\operatorname O(r)$, the column space is unchanged; uniqueness of the positive root gives $(Q_0^{\\mathsf T}Y^{\\mathsf T}YQ_0)^{-1/2}=Q_0^{\\mathsf T}(Y^{\\mathsf T}Y)^{-1/2}Q_0$, so the polar output is multiplied by $Q_0$. Thus the Grassmann outputs are well-defined.",
